replace(62,'The final student weight was supplied to model fitting during predictive tuning and final estimation, to validation and test metrics, and to both DML nuisance fits and the final residual estimator. Imputation, category-frequency processing, and standardisation were fitted on training observations without weighting.','Distinguish weighted fits from unweighted preprocessing')
replace(69,'We calculated SHAP values for the selected LightGBM model on up to 3,000 uniformly sampled training observations per plausible value. A generator initialised with seed 42 sampled observations without replacement sequentially across the ten analyses. We took the absolute value of each encoded-feature SHAP contribution, averaged these values using the sampled students\' final weights, summed them back to original variables, and averaged the results across ten plausible values. Sampling was uniform, rather than proportional to student weights.','Clarify sampling and absolute-value aggregation order')
replace(79,"Cross-fitting used three random student-level folds (seed 42), rather than school-grouped folds. Standard errors were clustered by school identifier (CNTSCHID), with a finite-cluster correction. The pooled variance was the mean within-plausible-value variance plus 1.1 times the sample variance of the ten estimates; confidence intervals used a normal approximation. We verified the availability of all 80 published replicate weights and the Fay-BRR and plausible-value pooling formulas (OECD, 2024). A separate weighted-mean calculation tested these formulas, but full replicate-specific refitting of the DML procedure was not implemented. The DML standard errors therefore do not represent complete design-based PISA uncertainty.",'Accurate cross-fitting, pooling and unresolved survey-variance scope')

before(81,'3.3.4 Supplementary Stability and Sensitivity Checks','Heading 3')
before(81,'We retained the original seed-42 analysis and added ten 80%/20% student-level splits using seeds 0, 1, 7, 13, 21, 73, 101, 202, 365, and 999. The 42 predictors remained fixed. For each split, training-only three-fold cross-validation selected from the original five LightGBM parameter candidates, followed by separate fits for all ten mathematics plausible values. Preprocessing was fitted within the relevant training partition. Model and SHAP random seeds remained 42. We summarised the ten split-level mean metrics using their mean, standard deviation, and range, and assessed SHAP ranks using top-ten frequencies and pairwise Spearman correlations. These summaries measure partition sensitivity, not population sampling uncertainty.')
before(81,'For MATHEFF, DISCLIM, TEACHSUP, BELONG, and ICTSCH, we replaced the LightGBM nuisance learners with ExtraTrees using 500 trees, a minimum leaf size of 3, a feature fraction of 0.8, and seed 42. Focal-factor-specific samples, adjustment variables, three-fold cross-fitting, student weights, school-clustered standard errors, and plausible-value pooling remained unchanged. We compared estimates, uncertainty, and direction without replacing the original results. These supplementary checks were post hoc, with settings fixed before execution; they were not preregistered. Full results and code are provided in Supplementary Robustness Checks.')
before(99,'As a supplementary case-sensitivity check, we excluded each of the twelve cases in turn and recalculated the mean paired difference for the primary outcome using the remaining eleven case-level differences. We retained all twelve exclusion results. The repeated generations were not treated as additional independent cases.')

